      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Barbers Point Naval Air Station, the Pacific’s largest during WWII, opened in 1942 and became a key launch point for carrier aircraft across the island.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kalaeloatown.com/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the 1999 closure, a 2006 master plan guided Hunt’s 2009‑2020 redevelopment, turning 4 million square feet of former base into housing, industry, and commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        11,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kalaeloatown.com/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Kalaeloa Heritage and Legacy Foundation partners with the HCDA and Native Hawaiian groups to protect cultural sites and teach local traditions.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kalaeloa_hawaii",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today Kalaeloa balances new development with respect for its layered cultural and historical heritage, exemplifying sustainable urban growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kalaeloa_hawaii",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Kalaeloa enjoys a tropical savanna climate, with every month above 18 °C and a pronounced dry season where the driest month drops below 60 mm.",
